      "passage": "Every Sunday, Rui En stood at her parents' wet market stall selling mutton. The stench of raw meat seeped into her clothes, and grease clung to her hands. She dreaded every moment, her mind swirling with images of horror etched on her classmates' faces if they ever discovered where she spent her weekends. \"Sue Yi,\" Rui En muttered to her sister, \"I really hope no one from school shows up.\" Sue Yi handed a bag of meat to a customer, her calm smile a stark contrast to the storm brewing in Rui En's chest. \"Rui En, there's nothing to be ashamed of,\" she murmured. But Rui En could not believe her. Each time Rui En looked around at the dangling cuts of meat, the anxiety of being exposed for being different hung over her like a heavy fog. Rui En's classmates spent weekends at malls and cafés, not in humid markets reeking of raw meat. The market was a constant reminder that Rui En was different. Yet deep down, Rui En knew she had no choice - her parents depended on her sister and her. (Then Rui En's heart lurched.) Eileen Tan - the most popular girl in class - appeared like a nightmare come true, weaving through the crowd with her phone held high, streaming live. Her nose wrinkled in disgust as she panned the camera across the slabs of meat. Rui En froze. A cold fear seeped into her bones. This was it - the moment her carefully hidden secret would unravel for the entire school to see. \"Sue Yi,\" Rui En hissed, flailing her arms to get Sue Yi's attention. But Sue Yi was busy with a customer, oblivious to the danger Rui En felt closing in. To Rui En's horror, Eileen's eyes met hers. A smirk spread across Eileen's face as she strutted right up to Rui En. \"Hey, Rui En,\" she sneered. \"Want to do something fun? Dump that bin of rubbish on your sister. I'm live - everyone will see.\" Rui En's hands started to tremble. She looked at the bin filled with slimy scraps. Her mind raced. She knew what she wanted most: If I did this, maybe Eileen would finally think I was cool enough to be her friend. Rui En grabbed the bin. It was heavier than she expected, the rotting smell stinging her eyes, making her legs wobble. Just then Sue Yi turned, and Rui En's and her eyes locked. Sue Yi's usual calm expression changed instantly - from calm to betrayed. \"Why are you letting her control you?\" Sue Yi asked quietly. Sue Yi's words struck Rui En in the face as shame crashed over her. \"What was I doing?\" Rui En dropped the bin with a thud, tears burning in her eyes. \"I'm so sorry, Sue Yi,\" Rui En whispered. But Sue Yi turned away, devastated. Suddenly, a shriek cut through the market. Eileen had slipped and fallen on the wet floor, crashing right into the bin Rui En had just dropped. Slime and scraps exploded across Eileen's white shirt. Her phone, still streaming, captured the humiliating moment as laughter erupted from the crowd. Eileen's cheeks crimson red as she scrambled to her feet, desperately trying to wipe the mess from her clothes. Shock flickered across Sue Yi's face. Then as Rui En and Sue Yi looked at each other, Sue Yi's expression softened and melted into laughter. Rui En felt the tension in her chest ease; she realised how close she had come to losing the one person who truly cared about her. As Rui En knelt to clean the mess, she found herself staring at a sheep's eyeball - round, glossy, unblinking. Rui En picked it up with trembling fingers, a strange calm settling over her. \"Hey, Rui En,\" Sue Yi called softly. Rui En looked up to see her lifting her phone, her eyes shining with warmth despite everything. \"Maybe it's time we come clean. Show everyone at school what life in the wet market is really like. The real us.\" \"Smile,\" Sue Yi said. Rui En forced a shaky grin, a swirl of embarrassment and relief surging inside her. Yet beneath it all, something inside her had shifted. Later that evening, Sue Yi posted the photo with the caption: EYE bring out the best in her! For the first time, Rui En felt the weight lift from her chest. She no longer cared what Eileen or anyone else thought. She had something far better - her family, and the courage to stand in her own skin. She looked at the unblinking eyeball and typed: EYE think so too. (Adapted from \"LIVE! At the Wet Market\")",
